    background_zh: '1950 年，数学家 Claude Shannon 在《哲学杂志》发表《计算机下棋程序》。彼时数字计算机刚起步，国际象棋因规则明确、信息完全，成为检验机器“智能”的理想试验场。Shannon 的动机并非造出世界冠军，而是证明“用机械搜索加评估函数”足以让机器下出有水平的棋。他首次把对弈形式化为博弈树，并指出穷举整局不现实，必须借助搜索与启发式剪枝。该文在尚无会下棋的程序之前，就预言了后来主导计算机棋类数十年的两条路线：暴力搜索（A 型）与选择性搜索（B 型）。',
    background_en: 'In 1950, mathematician Claude Shannon published "Programming a Computer for Playing Chess" in the Philosophical Magazine. Digital computers were still in their infancy, and chess—with its clear rules, perfect information, and decisive outcome—offered an ideal testbed for machine "intelligence." Shannon\'s aim was not to build a world champion but to show that mechanical search combined with an evaluation function could make a machine play a strong, human-like game. He was the first to formalize play as a game tree of positions and moves, and he argued that exhaustively analyzing an entire game was impossible. Instead, one must cut the tree at a fixed depth and judge leaf positions heuristically. Remarkably, before any chess program existed, Shannon predicted the two strategies that would dominate computer chess for decades: brute-force search (his Type A) and selective search (his Type B).',
