    technical_zh: 'Shannon 的核心思路是把“下棋”变成“在博弈树上做有界搜索”。每个局面是一个节点，每步合法走法是边；程序自当前局面向下展开若干层，到预设深度后用评估函数给叶节点打分，再按 minimax 自底向上回传：我方取最大、对手取最小。评估函数为加权求和——以子力价值为主，叠加机动性、王的安全、中心控制与兵形（叠兵、孤兵）等项。A 型搜索把每个分支展开到同一深度，简单稳妥，却因分支因子约 30 而指数膨胀（Shannon 估算只看三步就约 10^9 局面）。B 型先用启发式筛掉“不像样”的走法（强制着法、吃子、兑子），只深挖少数主线，并对激变局面补做“静止搜索”以免在交换中途误判。',
    technical_en: 'Shannon\'s core idea was to turn chess into a bounded search over a game tree. Each position is a node, each legal move an edge; the program expands downward from the current position for several plies, then scores the leaf positions with an evaluation function and backs the values up via minimax: we maximize, the opponent minimizes. Think of the leaf "terrain scores" as real, and the in-between scores as propagated under best/worst assumptions. The evaluation function is a weighted sum—material values dominate (rook 5, knight/bishop 3, pawn 1), augmented by mobility, king safety, central control, and pawn structure (doubled, isolated pawns). Type A expands every branch to a fixed depth: simple and reliable, yet it explodes combinatorially because the branching factor is about 30—Shannon estimated that even looking three moves ahead meant roughly 10^9 positions. Type B first filters out "unpromising" moves using heuristics (forcing moves, captures, exchanges), searches only a few main lines deeply, and applies a quiescence search to volatile leaves so it does not misjudge mid-exchange.',
    impact_zh: 'Shannon 的框架——状态表示、评估、搜索与启发式控制——成为一切博弈 AI 的基石，其 minimax 思想后来被 α-β 剪枝极大强化。但 A 型暴力搜索的算力需求随硬件缩放，反而长期压倒了更聪明的 B 型启发式；直到深蓝时代，纯搜索才战胜人类冠军。局限在于：固定深度评估会触发“地平线效应”，且下棋远未触及真正的理解。尽管如此，该文所确立的“以可观察行为而非内在意识衡量智能”的工程取向，与图灵测试一脉相承。',
    impact_en: 'Shannon\'s framework—state representation, evaluation, search, and heuristic control—became the bedrock of all game-playing AI, and his minimax idea was later greatly amplified by alpha-beta pruning. Ironically, the brute-force Type A scaled with hardware and long overshadowed the cleverer Type B heuristics; only with Deep Blue did pure search defeat a human champion. Its limit is the horizon effect from fixed-depth cutoffs, and chess never touched real understanding. Still, the paper\'s engineering stance—measuring intelligence by observable behavior—runs in the same spirit as the Turing Test.',
