    background_en: 'In 1952, IBM\'s Arthur Samuel wrote a checkers program for the IBM 701, one of the company\'s first commercial computers. A radio engineer by training rather than a chess master, he chose checkers over chess because its simpler rules and smaller branching factor let him focus on a bolder question: could a machine improve itself? The prevailing approach was hand-coded rules plus search, but Samuel wanted to test whether a program given an evaluation function and trial-and-error mechanisms could get better without being taught move by move by a human. This motivation directly gave rise to the very term and paradigm of "machine learning." Demonstrated on television in 1956, the program caused a sensation and reportedly nudged IBM\'s stock upward.',
