    technical_en: 'Samuel\'s program looked ahead a limited number of plies from the current position and scored terminal boards with a "scoring polynomial" (a linear function approximation). It followed Shannon\'s minimax: backing values up from scored leaves, each position receives the score of the best move assuming the opponent uses the same criterion—what Samuel called the backed-up score. Two learning mechanisms are the essence. First, rote learning stored each encountered position with its backed-up value; on reuse it cached prior searches, effectively deepening search, and a tiny discount per ply biased the program toward "win sooner, lose later." Second, generalization learning had two copies (Alpha, Beta) play many games and nudged feature weights so that successive position values stayed consistent—the prototype of later temporal-difference learning.',
